                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "4e0cecbe-4756-3ae7-8f6d-4a79c0a1d742",
                        "entry_id": "04c58939-425c-3ad1-8eb4-126c496d91be",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Généralisation du carré, de l’octaèdre et du 16-cellules à un espace euclidien de dimension quelconque. L’hyperoctaèdre à n dimension, ou le n-octaèdre, a 2n sommets dont les coordonnées peuvent être représentées par (±1, 0, …, 0), (0, ±1, …, 0), …, (0, 0, …, ±1)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "027df8bf-da4d-3524-a34b-c400e3de2c42",
                        "entry_id": "04c58939-425c-3ad1-8eb4-126c496d91be",
                        "glosses": [
                            "16-cellules. Polytope dont les seize faces tridimensionnelles sont des tétraèdres."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "especially"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "e6db34d2-1e92-4864-ba4b-8f9a7a35814c",
                                "sense_id": "027df8bf-da4d-3524-a34b-c400e3de2c42",
                                "text": "Mais il est la réunion de 5 hypericosaèdres disjoints, de 25 hypergranatoèdres disjoints, de 75 hyperoctaèdres disjoints et de 120 hypertétraèdres disjoints ! Tous les hypersolides réguliers sont inclus dans l’hyperdodécaèdre, même l’hypercube […]"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "3e82e4fe-53a1-4091-842f-08e1e5d85f58",
                        "source_entry_id": "04c58939-425c-3ad1-8eb4-126c496d91be",
                        "source_sense_description": "Généralisation du carré, de l’octaèdre et du 16-cellules",
                        "target_lang_word": "Kreuzpolytop",
                        "target_lang_code": "de",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": []
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "edb31fac-7e06-44e8-88bf-eeea31344369",
                        "source_entry_id": "04c58939-425c-3ad1-8eb4-126c496d91be",
                        "source_sense_description": "Généralisation du carré, de l’octaèdre et du 16-cellules",
                        "target_lang_word": "cross-polytope",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "cross-polytope",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "1523c884-7692-337b-8d01-871957a32b3f",
                                        "entry_id": "534782f9-57f1-3f7c-a799-7a43c169606c",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A polytope that is the convex hull of points, two on each Cartesian axis of a Euclidean space, that are equidistant from the origin; an orthoplex."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e1c9753d-dd24-4394-9ef5-76e4ab012162",
                                                "sense_id": "1523c884-7692-337b-8d01-871957a32b3f",
                                                "text": "The case p = 1, which is actually important to us, is illustrated in Figure 1.14; we are looking at the intersection of a cross-polytope with the probability simplex."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ffca4f90-76bb-4859-952e-4852e63a097b",
                                                "sense_id": "1523c884-7692-337b-8d01-871957a32b3f",
                                                "text": "2008, Robert Erdahl, Andrei Ordine, Konstantin Rybnikov, Perfect Delaunay Polytopes and Perfect Quadratic Functions on Lattices, Matthias Beck, et al. (editors), Contemporary Mathematics 452: Integer Points in Polyhedra — Geometry, Number Theory, Representation Theory, Algebra, Optimization, Statistics, AMS-IMS-SIAM Joint Summer Conference, June 11-15, 2006, page 104,\nThe convex hulls of such crosses often appear as cells in Delaunay tilings – cross polytopes are examples, as are the more spectacular symmetric perfect Delaunay polytopes."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "31c4bcf8-52ff-4f9a-9997-c14fa5ab4d76",
                                                "sense_id": "1523c884-7692-337b-8d01-871957a32b3f",
                                                "text": "The cross polytope βₙ is the regular polytope in n dimensions corresponding to the convex hull of the points formed by permuting the coordinates (±1,0,0,...,0), and has therefore 2n vertices. It is named so because its vertices are located equidistant from the origin, along the Cartesian axes in n-space. The cross polytope in n dimensions is bounded by 2ⁿ (n - 1)-simplices, has 2n vertices and 2ⁿ(n - 1) edges."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "664cedac-fd5d-46a0-8be5-f259ec69df5a",
                                                "sense_id": "1523c884-7692-337b-8d01-871957a32b3f",
                                                "text": "If one works with the l₁ norm, a ball (the set of all points whose distance lies within a certain radius around a point of interest) has the shape of a cross-polytope. A one-dimensional cross-polytope is a line segment, a two-dimensional cross-polytope is a square, for three dimensions, an octahedron, and so forth."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "84b46650-e0a8-4ebc-a501-4594aaed1657",
                                                "sense_id": "1523c884-7692-337b-8d01-871957a32b3f",
                                                "text": "While centrally symmetric d-polytopes with 2d vertices are affinely equivalent to the d-dimensional cross-polytope, a complete classification of the centrally symmetric d-polytopes with 2d + 2 vertices is out of reach."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "34bf01fc-4161-4e38-bd91-b81e76201dd2",
                        "source_entry_id": "04c58939-425c-3ad1-8eb4-126c496d91be",
                        "source_sense_description": "Généralisation du carré, de l’octaèdre et du 16-cellules",
                        "target_lang_word": "orthoplex",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "orthoplex",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "98349b52-0fff-3257-82fb-d42a3173612d",
                                        "entry_id": "0811897a-960a-32c0-ad23-57e573d7e214",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A convex polytope analogous to an octahedron (3 dimensions) or 16-cell (4 dimensions)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "db0a5e21-9ace-49a8-879d-ba66672cdd15",
                                                "sense_id": "98349b52-0fff-3257-82fb-d42a3173612d",
                                                "text": "We made use of another way of considering the 120 cells, starting with the eight at the vertices of an orthoplex, that is, in the cells of a hypercube."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0e95a38a-b016-4588-b0f9-c1c457182734",
                                                "sense_id": "98349b52-0fff-3257-82fb-d42a3173612d",
                                                "text": "It is remarkable that the four-dimensional orthoplex is the same polytope as the four-dimensional hemicube."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "bf981466-6f7e-431b-9a84-2d09f5512098",
                                                "sense_id": "98349b52-0fff-3257-82fb-d42a3173612d",
                                                "text": "The combinatorics of this case apply to all members of the Gosset series; in every case, their cells are simplexes and orthoplexes, the latter appearing with only half symmetry."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
